.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[CR]cbor::structs<T>\f[R] writes a C++ value as CBOR and reads it
back, with the types of a member and the description of a class.
The result allocates its strings and containers.
.PP
The examples use the types in test/doc_types.hpp, namespace
\f[CR]reference_structs\f[R].
.SS cbor::structs<T>::encode
Writes \f[CR]value\f[R] as one CBOR item in preferred serialization,
into a new \f[CR]std::string\f[R].
A struct is a map, and its keys come in the order of RFC 8949 4.2.1.
The second form appends to \f[CR]target\f[R] and returns the count of
bytes that it appended.
\f[CR]target\f[R] is a \f[CR]std::string\f[R], a container of
\f[CR]char\f[R], a \f[CR]std::span<char>\f[R] or a writer.
The function writes no shared value (tag 28, tag 29).
A type that \f[CR]structs\f[R] does not take does not compile.
.PP
\f[B]Parameters\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]target\f[R]: a \f[CR]std::string\f[R], a container of
\f[CR]char\f[R], a \f[CR]std::span<char>\f[R] or a writer
.SS cbor::structs<T>::decode
Reads one item into a \f[CR]T\f[R].
The result is a cbor::ref that holds the owner of the bytes, so a
\f[CR]std::string_view\f[R] in \f[CR]T\f[R] stays valid.
No form copies the bytes.
The first form moves an rvalue into a new owner; an lvalue does not
compile.
\f[CR]owner\f[R] keeps the bytes of a borrowed \f[CR]encoded\f[R] alive.
A form with \f[CR]target\f[R] reads into the containers of
\f[CR]target\f[R], with their allocator.
The other forms start from \f[CR]T{}\f[R].
A byte after the item is not read.
.PP
\f[B]Parameters\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]target\f[R]: the value whose containers and allocator the read
uses
.SS cbor::describe_members<T>
Describes how cbor::structs and cbor::schema write and read
\f[CR]T\f[R].
A specialization holds \f[CR]tag\f[R], a
\f[CR]std::optional<cbor::tag>\f[R], and \f[CR]members\f[R], a
\f[CR]std::tuple\f[R] of cbor::member entries.
\f[CR]cbor::untagged\f[R] gives a map with no tag.
The specialization stands outside every namespace, after \f[CR]T\f[R].
\f[CR]cbor::has_describe_members<T>\f[R] is true when \f[CR]T\f[R] has a
specialization.
.PP
A class that is not an aggregate, or that has a base class, needs a
description.
cbor::structs then reads the members, the keys and the tag from the
description only.
\f[CR]using base = B;\f[R] in the specialization names the direct base
class \f[CR]B\f[R]; cbor::schema takes a class with a base class only
so, and the members of \f[CR]B\f[R] stand in \f[CR]members\f[R] too.
\f[CR]CBOR_DEFINE_DERIVED_TYPE\f[R] and
\f[CR]CBOR_DEFINE_DERIVED_TYPE_MEMBERS\f[R] write such a specialization;
they take \f[CR]BASE\f[R] after \f[CR]T\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE(T, TAG, members...)\f[R] writes this
specialization.
The macro stands at global scope.
A type whose name holds a comma goes through an alias.
Where each key is the name of its member, CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE_MEMBERS takes
the names only.
.PP
\f[CR]decode\f[R] gives \f[CR]incorrect_type\f[R] for a missing tag or
another tag.
A description does not compile when it lists a member twice, gives two
members one key, names a member of another class, or has no
\f[CR]tag\f[R].
A tag of a standard type or an invalid tag does not compile.
With reflection, a list that differs from the public data members does
not compile.
An annotation on \f[CR]T\f[R] does not compile.
.SS cbor::member<P>
One entry of cbor::describe_members: the key of a member and a pointer
to that member.
The key is a text literal or an integer from \-2\(ha63 to 2\(ha64 \- 1.
The text is the name as it is written.
A pointer to a static member or to a member function does not compile.
A \f[CR]bool\f[R] or a character type as the key does not compile.
.PP
